<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chp-conditional-flow" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Conditional Flow</title>

	<introduction>
		<p>
				In the previous chapter, you learned to organize MATLAB commands into scripts and
				functions. While a script runs top to bottom without deviation, most real programs
				need to make decisions: take one action when a condition is true, and a different
				action when it is false. This chapter introduces the two primary decision-making
				tools in MATLAB: <term>if statements</term> and <term>switch statements</term>.
		</p>
		<p>
				Before writing if statements, you need to understand the values they test.
				This chapter builds from the ground up:
				<dl width="narrow">
						<li><title>Logical Values</title>
								<p>The building blocks of decisions; <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf> (stored as 1 and 0).</p>
						</li>
						<li><title>Logical Statements</title>
								<p>Comparisons and compound conditions formed with operators like <pf>==</pf>, <pf>&amp;&amp;</pf>, and <pf>||</pf>.</p>
						</li>
						<li><title>If Statements</title>
								<p>Conditional execution using <pf>if</pf>, <pf>elseif</pf>, and <pf>else</pf>.</p>
						</li>
						<li><title>Switch Statements</title>
								<p>Cleaner multi-way branching when selecting among exact values.</p>
						</li>
				</dl>
		</p>
		<p>
				By the end of this chapter you will be able to evaluate logical expressions,
				write if/elseif/else chains, and choose between if and switch based on the problem at hand.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<xi:include href="sec-logical-expressions.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="sec-if-statement.ptx" /> 
	<xi:include href="sec-switch-statement.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="exercises-conditional-flow.ptx" />

	<conclusion>
		<title>🗝️ Key Takeaways...</title>
		<assemblage xml:id="takeaways-conditional-flow">
			<p>
				<ul marker="square">
					<li>
						<term>Logical values</term>.
						MATLAB stores logical values as <pf>1</pf> (true) or <pf>0</pf> (false).
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><term>Relational operators</term>. Use relational operators</p>
						<tabular>
							<row><cell><pf>==</pf></cell><cell>equal to</cell><cell><pf>~=</pf></cell><cell>not equal to</cell></row>
							<row><cell><pf>&lt;</pf></cell><cell>less than</cell><cell><pf>&lt;=</pf></cell><cell>less than or equal to</cell></row>
							<row><cell><pf>&gt;</pf></cell><cell>greater than</cell><cell><pf>&gt;=</pf></cell><cell>greater than or equal to</cell></row>
						</tabular>
						<p>to create <term>logical expressions</term> that evaluate to <pf>1</pf> (true) or <pf>0</pf> (false).</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<term>Single equals vs. double equals</term>.
						A single equals (<pf>=</pf>) assigns a value to a variable, while a double equals (<pf>==</pf>) compares two values.
					</li>
					<li>
						<term>NOT operator</term>.
						Use <pf>~</pf> to negate a logical value or expression. For example, <pf>~(x &gt; 0)</pf> evaluates to true when x is not greater than 0.
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>Combining logical expressions</term>.
							Use <pf>&amp;</pf> for AND (both must be true) and <pf>|</pf> for OR (at least one must be true). For example:
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								(age &gt;= 18) &amp; hasLicense  % true only if both are true
								(x &lt; 0) | (x &gt; 100)       % true if either is true
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<term>Operator precedence</term>.
						When combining logical operators, NOT (<pf>~</pf>) is evaluated first, then AND (<pf>&amp;</pf>), then OR (<pf>|</pf>). Use parentheses to clarify complex expressions.
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><term>If statement</term>.</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								if condA
									blockA	% ⬅ runs if condA is true
								elseif condB
									blockB	% ⬅ runs if condB is true and condA is false
								else
									blockC	% ⬅ runs if condA and condB are both false
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><term>Switch statement</term>.</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								switch var
									case valueA
										blockA	% ⬅ runs if var matches valueA exactly
									case valueB
										blockB	% ⬅ runs if var matches valueB exactly
									otherwise
										blockC	% ⬅ runs if no cases above match
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>If vs. Switch</term>.
							Use <pf>switch</pf> to compare one variable against a small set of exact values (especially strings or integers).
							Use <pf>if</pf> when your conditions involve ranges, inequalities, or multiple variables.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</assemblage>
	</conclusion>

</chapter>